---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How QUIX Global s.r.o. processes personal data of Melveo users — transparently, per GDPR and Czech law."
  lang="en"
  noindex
>
  <PageHeader lang="en" />
  <article class="legal-article">
    <p class="legal-eyebrow">Upcoming version · Effective date to be announced</p>
    <h1 class="legal-h1">Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p><a href="/en/privacy/">Current version</a></p>
    <p class="legal-lead">
      This document describes which personal data we collect through the
      Melveo app and website, for what purposes, how long we retain it,
      and what your rights are. It is based on Regulation (EU) 2016/679
      (GDPR), Czech Act No. 110/2019 Coll., and related Czech legislation.
    </p>

    <hr />

    <h2>1. Operator and Data Controller</h2>
    <p>
      The data controller within the meaning of GDPR Art. 4(7) is:
    </p>
    <p class="legal-block">
      <strong>QUIX Global s.r.o.</strong><br />
      Company ID (IČO): 22466444<br />
      Registered office: Příčná 1892/4, Nové Město, 110 00 Prague 1, Czech
      Republic<br />
      Data box (datová schránka): <code>g7v78rx</code><br />
      Commercial Register: file C 416432, Municipal Court in Prague<br />
      <br />
      Contact: <a href="mailto:hello@melveo.app">hello@melveo.app</a>
    </p>
    <p>
      Given the size and nature of processing, the controller is not
      required to appoint a Data Protection Officer (DPO) under GDPR Art. 37.
      All data-related communication goes via the contact email above.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>App</strong> — Melveo mobile application for iOS/iPadOS.</li>
      <li><strong>Website</strong> — <code>melveo.app</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Player</strong> — natural person using the app as a
        player, alone or as a team member.</li>
      <li><strong>Coach</strong> — natural person using the app as a coach
        or staff member who runs a team.</li>
      <li><strong>Parent</strong> — legal guardian linked to a child under
        15 (Section 10).</li>
      <li><strong>Wellness check-in</strong> — daily self-rating
        (energy, sleep, soreness, mood, motivation) on a 1–5 scale.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. What personal data we process</h2>

    <h3>3.1. Identifiers</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>First and last name</li>
      <li>Email address</li>
      <li>User identifier (UUID generated by the system)</li>
      <li>Role (player, coach) and function in a team (founder, admin,
        member)</li>
      <li>Optional: profile photo, locale (cs/en)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.2. Sport activity data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Wellness check-ins — five numeric values 1–5 with timestamp</li>
      <li>Optional pain notes; player-private notes</li>
      <li>Training and match attendance, events, lineups, statistics, and reports</li>
      <li>Subjective perceived load (RPE) on a 1–10 scale post-session</li>
      <li>Optional personality profile and team communication preferences</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Some sports and wellness signals (for example pain, symptoms, HRV,
      or data from health integrations) may, in context, reveal health-related
      information. We process such data only to the extent necessary for team
      sports operations, with role-based access controls, and optional health
      integrations only with explicit consent.
    </p>

    <h3>3.3. Device and sensor data (optional, only with consent)</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Apple Health: HRV, resting and activity heart rate, steps, completed
        workouts, active energy, distances and effort ratings explicitly shared
        with the app. The app only reads these data. Sleep is entered manually
        in check-ins; this version does not import it from Apple Health.</li>
      <li>Polar (optional): morning HRV measurement and heart rate during activity</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.4. User content and media</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Optional profile and team photo</li>
      <li>Free-form notes in check-ins, training sessions, matches, and reports</li>
      <li>Optional match video, its technical metadata, and generated clips</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Match video is not used for biometric identification or advertising.
      It is available to authorized coaching staff and active players of the
      same team in a completed report. After access is revoked, no new
      playback link can be obtained; a short-lived link already issued may
      still work until it expires.
    </p>

    <h3>3.5. Messages (team chat)</h3>
    <p>
      Only members of the same team can message each other. Text, reactions,
      read receipts and attachments (photos, videos, documents) are end-to-end
      encrypted with the MLS protocol on the device. Melveo servers cannot read
      their content.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>To deliver messages, the server processes who is in which
        conversation, which account and device sent a message, the time, the
        size and the devices' public keys. Notifications contain no message
        text, name or preview.</li>
      <li>The server keeps encrypted attachments only until every recipient
        has downloaded them, for at most 45 days. History stays on the
        devices.</li>
      <li>Backup: the recovery key is stored in your iCloud Keychain and never
        reaches Melveo. Message history is backed up to the Melveo server
        encrypted with this key, photos and videos to your private iCloud
        storage. Without the key nobody can read the backup, Melveo included.</li>
      <li>Reports: you send Melveo support the text of the messages you
        select, the fingerprint and size of attachments (not the file), the
        reason, an optional note and a cryptographic proof of who sent the
        message. Only Melveo support reads reports, never the coach or the
        team.</li>
      <li>Blocking: the server stores whom you blocked so that the blocked
        person cannot message you privately.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.6. Operational and technical data</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Login time, IP address (processed only for fraud detection,
        retained max 30 days)</li>
      <li>A random installation identifier for request correlation, security
        auditing, and duplicate handling; it is never used for advertising</li>
      <li>Optional limited product-use events (for example opening an overview
        or completing a check-in), linked to the account. They are stored only
        after explicit in-app consent and never contain wellness answers, HRV,
        pain, notes, or another person's data.</li>
      <li>MetricKit diagnostics stay on the device. They leave the device only
        if the user explicitly exports and sends them to support.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.7. Subscription and payment data</h3>
    <p>
      When subscriptions launch, Stripe will process website purchases and
      Apple will process App Store purchases. Public sales are in preparation.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Website purchases:</strong> plan, status, period, price and
        invoices, Stripe customer and subscription IDs, who pays and who
        receives the subscription (for a parent buying for a child, the parent
        and the child) and the payer's confirmation of being at least 15 or
        paying as the adult legal guardian of a child under 15, and the request to start performance within
        the withdrawal period. Card data are seen only by Stripe, not by
        us.</li>
      <li><strong>In-app purchases:</strong> payment is processed by
        <strong>Apple as an independent controller</strong> under its own
        privacy policy. We do not receive your Apple ID, payment method or
        address. From Apple we receive only transaction data: transaction ID,
        product, purchase date and period end, renewal status, whether it is a
        trial, App Store country, price and any refund. To match the purchase
        to your account, the app passes Apple a random purchase account
        identifier. We send Apple no other data about you.</li>
      <li><strong>Free trial:</strong> we record trial use in the player or
        coach plan family to check eligibility on the Website. Apple
        determines eligibility for introductory offers in the App Store.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3.8. Cookies and similar technologies</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>Necessary storage — for example saving your consent choice in the
        browser (<code>melveo:consent</code>) and security or operational
        features of the website.</li>
      <li>Cloudflare Web Analytics — if enabled, we use cookieless aggregate
        analytics without storing identifying cookies.</li>
      <li>Google Analytics — only after explicit consent in the Analytics
        category.</li>
      <li>Meta Pixel — only after explicit consent in the Marketing category.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can change your consent at any time through "Cookie settings" in
      the page footer. Necessary technologies cannot be disabled because the
      website needs them to operate safely and consistently.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Purpose of processing and legal basis (GDPR Art. 6)</h2>
    <table class="legal-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Legal basis (GDPR Art. 6)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Providing the service</td>
          <td>Contract performance — Art. 6(1)(b)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>User communication and support</td>
          <td>Legitimate interest — Art. 6(1)(f)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Subscriptions on the website and in the App Store, matching a
            purchase to the account, refunds</td>
          <td>Contract performance — Art. 6(1)(b)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>One free trial per person and plan family</td>
          <td>Legitimate interest — Art. 6(1)(f)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Statutory obligations (accounting)</td>
          <td>Legal obligation — Art. 6(1)(c)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Analytics and product improvement</td>
          <td>Consent — Art. 6(1)(a) for optional in-app product analytics and
            cookie-based website tools; legitimate interest — Art. 6(1)(f)
            only for cookieless aggregate website analytics</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Marketing communication</td>
          <td>Consent — Art. 6(1)(a)</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Apple Health / Polar integration and sensitive wellness signals</td>
          <td>Consent — Art. 6(1)(a) + Art. 9(2)(a)</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>5. Data aggregation principle (privacy by design)</h2>
    <p>
      Team coaches work with <strong>team-level overviews and
      signals</strong> (averages, submission counts, warning flags). They
      see a player's individual health data only to the extent the player
      (under 15, the parent) allows sharing. These rules are enforced at the
      database level (RLS policies and SECURITY DEFINER RPC functions).
    </p>
    <p>
      A player has access to their own data and, as an active team member,
      to the completed report’s summary, timeline, and video. Heart-rate
      data in the team report are visible only to coaching staff. A coach can
      allow a player to add and edit their own tags in a specific report;
      players cannot change other people’s tags, the shared video source,
      or time alignment. Revoking this permission prevents further edits.
      Melveo has no clubs or club licences, so no organisation above the
      team has access to the data.
    </p>
    <p>
      Melveo does not provide outputs that by themselves constitute legally
      binding or similarly significant automated decisions under GDPR Art. 22.
      App outputs serve as decision-support context for coaches and staff.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Retention</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Identifiers: for the lifetime of the account. Deleting the account
        removes the account and data linked to its identity, except records
        that must be retained for a legal reason as listed below.</li>
      <li>Wellness check-ins and personal sports data: while the account is
        used or the person remains a team member. Account deletion removes
        them or irreversibly separates them from the identity according to
        ownership of the relevant team record.</li>
      <li>Accounting and billing data: 10 years per Czech VAT Act
        No. 235/2004 Coll. and Accounting Act No. 563/1991 Coll.</li>
      <li>Subscription and transaction data from the website and the App
        Store: for the subscription and 3 years after it ends, for complaints
        and refunds</li>
      <li>Operational data (IP, crash logs): max 30 days</li>
      <li>Product-use events: only while consent remains active; withdrawing
        consent in the app immediately deletes the account's existing events</li>
      <li>Match video and generated clips: until deleted by an authorized user
        or the relevant team workspace is closed. After a deletion request,
        the source and derived objects are removed by a controlled deletion
        process; only a short-lived technical record remains to finish and
        audit that deletion.</li>
      <li>Messages: on the participants' devices until they delete them.
        Encrypted attachments on the server until every recipient has
        downloaded them, for at most 45 days.</li>
      <li>Message reports: 12 months from submission, then deleted. Longer
        only for a report that support preserves as evidence, for example for
        the police.</li>
      <li>Messages after account deletion: the server deletes your devices,
        keys and encrypted history backup. Messages already delivered to
        others stay with them under Deleted account; they cannot be removed
        from other people's devices. The app deletes the recovery key from
        iCloud Keychain and the encrypted photo and video backups from your
        iCloud when you delete the account on a device signed in to iCloud.
        Otherwise you delete them yourself in iCloud Settings.</li>
      <li>Cookie consent: until withdrawn or until the consent schema version
        changes</li>
      <li>Analytics and marketing data: per the tool's settings (Google
        Analytics: 14 months, Meta Pixel: typically 90 days)</li>
    </ul>

